Andrew Dolan said:

@Bas: So the attack scenario is limited to posing as a webserver that was issued a legitimate certificate. As long as you have a classical signing key, an adversary can recover it and is off to the races.

An un-upgraded webserver is never secure. That's not what we're solving.

Andrew Dolan said:

@Bas: So the attack scenario is limited to posing as a webserver that was issued a legitimate certificate. As long as you have a classical signing key, an adversary can recover it and is off to the races.

This isn't really true though.  As long as a client trusts a CA that has traditional crypto signing in play, a CRQC can break its key and generate a new certificate for that server.  It doesn't have to be related to any CA in use by the server.  Assuming that the client is trusting only via the certificate it receives on the wire and the attacker has any control over that.

@Christopher

As long as a client trusts a CA that has traditional crypto signing in play, a CRQC can break its key and generate a new certificate for that server.

Agreed. This was my point (just not as well written as you put it here).
